                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/monoid+theory+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Definition"} Definition
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#further_weakening"} Further weakening
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            %em rig
            one means a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+structure"} mathematical structure
            much like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            but without the assumption that every element has an additive inverse, hence without the assumption of
            %em negatives
            (whence the omission of “n” from “ring” &amp;lbrack;
            %a{:href => "#Schanuel91"}> Schanuel 1991 p. 379
            ,
            = succeed "&rbrack;)" do
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere92"} Lawvere 1992 p. 2
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (Terminology: Rigs and semirings)
              Rigs are commonly also called
              = succeed "," do
                %em semirings
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiring"} semiring
              for a discussion about the various definitions of semirings; only one of the proposed definitions is the same as the one of
              %em rigs
              as considered here.
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %em rig
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            with binary operations of addition and multiplication, such that
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
              under multiplication;
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
              under addition;
            %li
              multiplication distributes over addition, i.e. the distributivity laws hold:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\cdot (y+z) = (x\cdot y) + (x\cdot z)
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo +
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y+z)\cdot x = (y\cdot x) + (z\cdot x)
              %p and also the absorption laws, which are the nullary version of the distributive laws:
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi x
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mn 0
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\cdot x = 0 = x\cdot 0.
          %p
            In a ring, the absorption laws follow from distributivity, since for example
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo +
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi x
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\cdot x + 0\cdot x = (0+0)\cdot x = 0\cdot x
            and we can cancel one copy to obtain
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi x
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0\cdot x = 0
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig"} rig
          %p
            More sophisticatedly, we can say that just as a ring is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            in
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            that has a universal property for bilinear maps.
          %p
            Equivalently, a rig is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            of a category with a single object that is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            in the category of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoids"} commutative monoids
          %p
            Rigs and rig
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            form the category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rig"} Rig
          %h3#further_weakening Further weakening
          %p
            As with rings, one sometimes considers non-associative or non-unital versions (where multiplication may not be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative"} associative
            or may have no
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            can be proved (from the other axioms) to be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            under addition (and therefore a ring) as long as it is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near-ring"} near-ring
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            Many rigs are either
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
            = succeed "))." do
              %a{:href => "#Golan2003"} Golan 2003, Proposition 2.25
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+algebra"} Boolean algebra
            is a rig in both ways: as a lattice and as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+ring"} Boolean ring
          %p
            Any rig can be “completed” to a ring by adding negatives, in generalization of how the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            are completed to the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+classes"} isomorphism classes
            of objects in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
          %p
            More formally, the ring completion of a rig
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is obtained by applying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            functor to the underlying additive monoid of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi Rig
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ring
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: Rig \to Ring
            to the forgetful functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mi Ring
                    %mo →
                    %mi Rig
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: Ring \to Rig
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \to U F(R)
            is not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monic
            if the additive monoid of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is not
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                cancellative
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cancellation+monoid"} ?
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/completion"} completion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            where the unit
            %em is
            monic.
          %p
            Matrices of rigs can be used to formulate versions of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+mechanics"} matrix mechanics
          %p
            Every rig with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+characteristic"} positive characteristic
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p Some rigs which are neither rings nor distributive lattices include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers"} natural numbers
            %li
              %p
                The nonnegative
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+number"} rational number
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %li
              %p Polynomials with coefficients in any rig.
            %li
              %p
                The set of isomorphism classes of objects in any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+category"} distributive category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
            %li
              %p
                The
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tropical+rig"} tropical rig
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}\cup \{\infty\}
                with addition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi y
                      %mo =
                      %mi min
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\oplus y = min(x,y)
                and multiplication
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi y
                        %mo =
                        %mi x
                        %mo +
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\otimes y = x+y
              %p
                Tropical rigs are among the important class of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+semirings"} idempotent semirings
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ideals"} ideals
                of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
                form a rig under ideal addition and multiplication, where the unit and zero ideals are the unit and zero elements of the rig, respectively. They also form a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+lattice"} distributive lattice
                and therefore a rig in another way; note that the addition operation is the same in both rigs but the multiplication operation is different (being intersection in the lattice).
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semiring"} semiring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rng"} rng
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nonunital+ring"}> nonunital ring
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"}> ring
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near-ring"} near-ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tropical+geometry"}> tropical geometry
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tropical+semiring"} tropical semiring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+semiring"} idempotent semiring
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorification"} categorification
                of the notion of rig is the notion of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colax-distributive+rig+category"} colax-distributive rig category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-rig"} 2-rig
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributivity+for+monoidal+structures"} distributivity for monoidal structures
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-monoid"}> semi-monoid
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-group"} semi-group
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-category"}> semi-category
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-Segal+space"} semi-Segal space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-simplicial+set"} semi-simplicial set
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Burnside+rig"} Burnside rig
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/division+rig"} division rig
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicatively+cancellable+rig"} multiplicatively cancellable rig
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasiregular+rig"} quasiregular rig
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Conway+rig"} Conway rig
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleene+star+algebra"} Kleene star algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additively+idempotent+rig"} additively idempotent rig
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicatively+idempotent+rig"} multiplicatively idempotent rig
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+rig+%28Guzm%C3%A1n%29"} Boolean rig (Guzmán)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+of+a+rig"} characteristic of a rig
          %h2#references References
          %p The terminology “rig” is due to:
          %ul
            %li#Schanuel91
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephen+H.+Schanuel"}> Stephen H. Schanuel
                , p. 379 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Negative sets have Euler characteristic and dimension
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Category Theory
                %strong 1488
                (1991) 379–385 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0084232"}> doi:10.1007/BFb0084232
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Lawvere92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"}> William Lawvere
                , pp. 1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to Linear Categories and Applications
                = succeed "," do
